# Heap-loaded wss:443 handshake gate (FUG-133 / the PR #114 OOM class) against the
# esp32c6_netstack drop-in. Reserve, flash, provision, then LOAD the device to its
# field worst case — a full 768-LED (kMaxLeds) map + an activated texture-sampling
# effect + a resident streamed texture keyframe — over wss, then DROP that socket so
# its ~6 KB TLS session frees while the map/effect/texture stay RAM-resident. Then
# open a FRESH wss:443 session on the loaded heap and complete hello/welcome (this is
# the exact #114 alloc the gate exists to guard) followed by a SEQUENTIAL cert-page
# GET / (the netstack TLS server is single-connection — it sheds concurrent SYNs, so
# a concurrent second session is untestable). Gates on RECOVERY (a clean handshake +
# 200 within the recover window), reusing tls_churn_core's verdict; the mbedTLS-alloc
# serial scan is informational, never the gate. A manual+hitl py_test; --device-ws
# hits a reachable board directly (skips reserve/flash/provision). Pure verdict logic
# lives in loaded_tls_core + tls_churn_core and is unit-tested in //pi/hitl/tests.
